the game could not be played or finished.\",\n \"The attract-mode soak plus a scripted full playthrough with debug snapshots: it drove the cook, the laundry, the fight and the boss through the real scenes and printed the state at each step; the AI Hub harness came a close second.\",\n \"Static screenshots of the title at several viewport sizes: they found no layout problems, because the engine's expand mode already handled every shape.\"],\n\"engineFeedback\":[\n \"The look comes almost free: cutaway TileMap walls, shaded Humanoid rigs with outfits, materials, canvas lighting and particles, plus the pause menu, settings, controls rebinding, dialog with portraits and the HTML HUD, so the time went into design and not plumbing. The fixed step made headless, repeatable tests easy.\",\n \"Yes. E.prune removes the items the callback returns true for, which I read as keep. A one-line index entry was not enough there. Settings in the 'Controller' group show on the Controls screen and not Settings, and the font has no middle dot. I also guessed that the dialog voice could be per speaker; it takes one effect name, so I read the text as it types and play my own blips.\",\n \"The game's brain: the story as scripts over dialog, the cop's sight and suspicion, the goons and the boss's telegraphed charge, the cook and laundry minigames, the attract-mode AI that plays through them, the gibberish voices, the stick-flip patch for the Settings toggles, the touch-only notice and the AI Hub ghost sync.\",\n \"Working out what a call means from one line: prune's direction, the dialog's internals for typed-text sounds, and which direction a screen vector becomes on the ground in each view when an AI has to steer.\",\n \"The prune helper: rename it removeIf or make the predicate a keep test, so the call reads like what it does.\",\n \"Give every game a debug handle on window by default (the scene, the state snapshot, a go() and a teleport), because every browser test had to add one by hand